---
import Doc from "../../layouts/Doc.astro";
import MethodologyNav from "../../components/MethodologyNav.astro";
import MethodFacts from "../../components/MethodFacts.astro";
import SubcheckTable from "../../components/SubcheckTable.astro";
import { M, axisByKey, percent } from "../../lib/methodology";

const axis = axisByKey("auth_posture");
const transportShare = percent(
  M.subcheck_weights.auth_posture.find((s) => s.key === "transport_security")!.weight,
);
const notes = {
  authentication_model: "OAuth 2.0 or a robust API key, required, documented, and refused without credentials.",
  transport_security: "stdio, or HTTPS-only with HSTS. Read from the registry entry, so it is measured even when no source is reachable.",
  secret_handling: "No credentials committed; secrets read from the environment and documented. Needs source.",
  authorization_granularity: "Per-tool or per-resource access control. Scored only where the methodology's heuristic decides the case; otherwise not assessed.",
};
---

<Doc
  title="Auth Posture — mcpwatchman methodology"
  description="How mcpwatchman's Auth Posture axis scores MCP servers: authentication model, transport security, secret handling and authorization granularity, with every band."
  canonical="https://mcpwatchman.com/methodology/auth-posture/"
  heading="Auth Posture"
  crumb={{ href: "/methodology/", label: "Methodology" }}
>
  <Fragment slot="lede">
    <p>
      How a server authenticates its clients and protects its credentials.
      Provisional weight {percent(axis.weight)}%. This is a property of the
      server's interface, not its code patterns, so it is kept apart from Code
      Safety: perfect code can still sit behind an unauthenticated HTTP port.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
  <MethodFacts slot="facts" />

  <MethodologyNav />

  <section class="paper doc-paper" aria-labelledby="sub-heading">
    <h2 id="sub-heading">Sub-checks</h2>
    <p>
      The axis is the weighted mean of the sub-checks that could be assessed.
      One that could not is left out and its weight renormalised away, and
      the axis reports the share that was measured.
    </p>
    <SubcheckTable axis="auth_posture" caption="Auth Posture sub-checks" notes={notes} />

    <h3>Authentication model</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="ledger">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col" class="num">Score</th><th scope="col">When</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="num">100</td><td>stdio transport. The client launches the server as a subprocess, so the operating-system process boundary is the authentication boundary, and absent auth is not penalised.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">100</td><td>Network transport, and the source enforces a credential: the request is refused without one.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">80</td><td>A credential is required but weakly validated; or an authentication library is imported with no call to it found; or the registry declares a required credential header.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">60</td><td>Authentication is present but optional or trivially bypassed, or the source reads a credential header with no enforcement found.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">30</td><td>Network transport and no authentication in code that serves the endpoint.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The 30 band is an accusation, so it is only established from code that
      serves the endpoint. A server whose network transport is declared only
      as a hosted remote is not accused from its repository's silence; the
      sub-check abstains. A detector must show that the server checks a
      credential, not that a credential-shaped name appears: a header the
      server sends, an import nothing uses, or a token request to somebody
      else's OAuth server is not authentication.
    </p>

    <h3>Transport security</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="ledger">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col" class="num">Score</th><th scope="col">When</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="num">100</td><td>stdio, or HTTPS-only with HSTS.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">80</td><td>HTTPS-only endpoints. HSTS can only be seen by connecting, which the scanner never does, so this band is the ceiling for a remote server today — an unverifiable band, not an observed absence.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">50</td><td>Both HTTP and HTTPS endpoints declared.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">20</td><td>Plaintext HTTP only.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      An HTTP server with no authentication and no TLS falls to the 20–30
      range. That is the single highest-impact misconfiguration in the
      ecosystem, and the score says so plainly.
    </p>

    <h3>Secret handling</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="ledger">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col" class="num">Score</th><th scope="col">When</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="num">0</td><td>A committed credential in a provider format — an AWS key, a GitHub token, a PEM private key.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">100</td><td>Secrets are read from the environment and the README documents them.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">70</td><td>Secrets are read from the environment but not documented.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      A credential's shape is not a credential. Matches from keyword, entropy
      or basic-auth heuristics alone flag placeholders, variable names and
      hashes as readily as real secrets, so they are listed and the sub-check
      is left unassessed, as a limit of ours. Matches in test code are listed
      and never scored.
    </p>

    <h3>Authorization granularity</h3>
    <p>
      The methodology's heuristic asks whether a server exposes more than{" "}
      {M.granularity_tool_threshold} tools, and whether any conditional access
      logic exists. Two outcomes are decided and score 100: at most{" "}
      {M.granularity_tool_threshold} tools, so there is nothing to segregate;
      or more, with conditional access logic present. The methodology assigns
      no band to the remaining case — many tools, no access logic, “needs
      human review” — so it is not assessed rather than given an invented
      number. Neither is a server whose tools could not be counted.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section" aria-labelledby="partial-heading">
    <h2 id="partial-heading">Why this axis is often partly measured</h2>
    <p>
      Transport security needs only the registry entry, and every other
      sub-check needs source. A server whose repository cannot be read
      therefore often scores on transport security alone —{" "}
      {transportShare}% of the axis. The meter draws the rest hatched, and the score is printed with
      its measured share, never on its own.
    </p>
  </section>
</Doc>
